00:23:37,870 --> 00:23:43,510
paintings by many world -famous artists like Monet and Picasso.

236
00:23:44,190 --> 00:23:48,590
Its headquarters houses some 3000 works of art.

237
00:23:49,450 --> 00:23:54,410
The main building is topped with a Georgian tower with four sculptures of the

238
00:23:54,410 --> 00:23:58,110
mythical winged horse, the magazine's corporate logo.

239
00:23:58,990 --> 00:24:04,910
It sits on 127 acres of well -trimmed lawns.

240
00:24:04,910 --> 00:24:09,510
The editor's office used to be occupied by founder DeWitt Wallace,

241
00:24:10,550 --> 00:24:17,970
along with his wife Leila Acheson Wallace, launched Reader's Digest in 1922 with

242
00:24:17,970 --> 00:24:20,630
condensed articles from other publications.

243
00:24:21,710 --> 00:24:27,930
It has become the world's most widely read magazine, selling 28 million copies each

244
00:24:27,930 --> 00:24:32,510
month in 17 languages and 41 different editions.

245
00:24:32,510 --> 00:24:39,470
The Wallaces, both children of church ministers, had a clearly defined formula for

246
00:24:39,470 --> 00:24:43,600
their little magazine, as Reader's Digest was originally subtitled.

247
00:24:44,730 --> 00:24:48,510
Articles were to be short, readable, and uplifting.

248
00:24:49,690 --> 00:24:52,810
Subjects were picked to inspire or entertain.

249
00:24:53,570 --> 00:24:57,130
The Wallaces didn't accept advertising in the U.S.

250
00:24:57,190 --> 00:25:04,590
edition until 1955, and even then they didn't allow any ads for cigarettes, liquor,

251
00:25:04,670 --> 00:25:10,850
or drugs. The Wallaces also had a clear sense of the kind of workplace they wanted.

252
00:25:12,130 --> 00:25:17,290
It started as a mom -and -pop operation, and the childless Wallaces always

253
00:25:17,290 --> 00:25:20,230
considered employees to be part of their family.

254
00:25:21,170 --> 00:25:25,910
Employees still tell stories of how the Wallaces would take care of employees who

255
00:25:25,910 --> 00:25:31,530
had met with misfortunes, and they showered their employees with unusual benefits,

256
00:25:32,090 --> 00:25:35,710
like a turkey at Thanksgiving and Fridays off in May.

257
00:25:36,350 --> 00:25:39,490
This cozy workplace no longer exists here.

258
00:25:40,390 --> 00:25:44,810
The Wallaces both died in their 90s in the early 1980s.

259
00:25:45,110 --> 00:25:52,010
George Groon, a former ad salesman who joined Reader's Digest in 1960, has his eye

260
00:25:52,010 --> 00:25:53,690
focused on the bottom line.

261
00:25:53,690 --> 00:25:58,010
In a few short years, he turned the magazine on its head.

262
00:25:58,530 --> 00:26:00,990
laid off several hundred workers.

263
00:26:01,430 --> 00:26:07,930
Especially hard hit were the blue and pink collar departments, such as fulfillment.

